<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<speak version="1.0" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis" xml:lang="en-US">

  <!-- 1. Introduction -->

    Meet Super Jump, a free, open source plugin for Intelli-J Idea.
    It turns any text in your editor into a launchpad.
    Put your cursor on something, press a shortcut, and Super Jump takes you straight to the right place on the web.
    No copying, no pasting, no searching.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 2. Installation -->

    Installing is simple.
    Open Settings, go to Plugins, then the Marketplace tab, and search for Super Jump.
    Click Install, and you are ready to go.
    You can also download it from the JetBrains Marketplace website, or from the GitHub releases page.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 3. The two actions and their shortcuts -->

    Super Jump gives you two actions.
    The first is Super Jump, on Control Shift 9.
    It looks at the text under your cursor and works out where you probably want to go.
    The second is Super Jump Git, on Control Shift 0.
    It takes you to the current file and line in your Git repository.
    Both shortcuts can be changed under Settings, Keymap, Plugins, Super Jump.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 4. SuperJump: URLs -->

    Let's start with the basics.
    Put your cursor on a web address and press Control Shift 9.
    Your default browser opens right at that page.
    It works on plain addresses, and on links inside markdown too.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 5. SuperJump: CVEs -->

    Spotted a vulnerability identifier in a comment or a log file?
    Put your cursor on it, and jump.
    Super Jump opens the vulnerability report, by default on the Sonatype guide, so you can read the details straight away.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 6. SuperJump: colours -->

    Got a hex colour in your stylesheet?
    Jump from it, and Super Jump opens a colour information page, showing shades, conversions, and matching palettes.
    It works with both lower case and upper case values.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 8. SuperJump: repositories -->

    Mention of a GitHub repository, written as owner slash name?
    Jump, and you land on that repository on GitHub.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 9. SuperJump: Jira tickets -->

    Ticket numbers in your comments and commit notes become one keystroke away.
    Put your cursor on a ticket key, and Super Jump opens it in your own Jira.
    This is the one setting you need to configure, because Super Jump needs the address of your Jira instance.
    We will do that shortly.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 10. SuperJump: Linux commands -->

    Super Jump also understands Linux commands.
    Put your cursor on a command such as ls or grep, and it opens the documentation for it, by default on TLDR.
    In markdown files, Super Jump is careful.
    In a plain code block, the command is not matched.
    In a block marked as bash, it is.
    The same applies to Groovy dependencies and Docker files: mark the code block with its language, and Super Jump knows what you mean.
    Shell scripts work directly, as you can see in the example dot S H file.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 7. SuperJump: dependencies -->

    This one saves a lot of time.
    In a Gradle build file, put your cursor on a dependency line and jump.
    Super Jump opens that library on Maven Central, including the exact version if you specified one.
    If there is no version, you get the library's main page.
    It also works on the bare group, name, and version form, without the implementation wrapper.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 11. SuperJump Git -->

    Now for Super Jump Git.
    Imagine you are in a file called EXAMPLE dot S H, on line forty two, and you want to share that exact spot with a colleague.
    Press Control Shift 0, and your browser opens the same file, on the same line, in your Git repository.
    Copy the address and send it.
    Done.

  <break time="700ms"/>

    It supports GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket.
    Other hosts get GitHub style links.
    S S H remotes are converted to H T T P S automatically.
    If you are on a detached checkout, the link points to the exact commit, so it never goes stale.
    File paths and branch names are safely encoded.
    And when you have text selected, Super Jump Git matches the whole selection, rather than just the caret position.

  <break time="700ms"/>

    The lookup runs in the background, so your editor never freezes.
    If something goes wrong, you get a clear error message.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 12. The Git fallback -->

    What if Super Jump does not recognise the text?
    By default, it falls back to Super Jump Git, so you still land somewhere useful: your repository, at the current file and line.
    For example, if your cursor is on a blank line, there is nothing to match, so you simply go to GitHub.
    If you prefer it to do nothing, you can turn this off in the settings.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 13. Settings and custom jumps -->

    Everything is configurable.
    Open Settings, Tools, Super Jump.
    First, enter your Jira address in the Jira U R L field.
    Below that is the list of jumpers.
    Each one pairs a regular expression, which recognises the text, with a web address, which says where to go.

  <break time="700ms"/>

    The regular expressions need at least one group, and groups are surrounded by round brackets.
    The first pair of brackets is group one, the second pair is group two, and so on.
    The Gradle coordinates jumper has three parts, so it recognises groups one, two, and three.
    The web address then substitutes one, two, and three to build the page to jump to.

  <break time="700ms"/>

    You can also restrict a jumper to specific files.
    For example, you might only want to recognise a command when you are in a Docker file, in which case it takes you to Docker Hub.
    You could add Gradle there too, for build dot gradle dot K T S, or plain build dot gradle if it is a regular Groovy build file.

  <break time="700ms"/>

    Change any address to suit you.
    Perhaps you prefer a different vulnerability database, or your own internal documentation site.
    You could even point ticket numbers at GitHub issues instead of Jira.
    Add new entries for patterns unique to your company.
    Use the arrows to move entries up and down, because the first match wins.
    And if you ever get tangled up, Reset to defaults puts everything back.

  <break time="700ms"/>

    If one of your regular expressions is invalid, Super Jump simply skips it, so a typo never breaks every other jump.

  <break time="1s"/>

  <!-- 14. Selection behaviour and notifications -->

    Super Jump uses your selection if you have one.
    Otherwise it uses the word under the cursor, or the whole line.
    When something cannot be opened, or a web address is missing, you get a friendly Super Jump notification, instead of silent failure.

  <break time="2s"/>

  <!-- 15. IdeaVim users -->

    Vim users, you have not been forgotten.
    Add two lines to your dot ideavimrc file: nmap g j to the Super Jump action, and nmap g x to the Super Jump Git action.
    Now it is just two keystrokes in normal mode.

  <break time="2s"/>

  <!-- 16. Wrap up -->

    That is Super Jump.
    One shortcut to leave your editor for the right page, and a second to share exactly where you are in your code.
    It is free and open source, and contributions are very welcome, especially ideas for new jump patterns.
    Find it on the JetBrains Marketplace, and report issues or suggestions on GitHub.
    Thanks for watching, and happy jumping.

</speak>
